{"sounds":{"intro":"Chagatai is the classical literary language of Central Asia, the language of Navoi and Babur. It is the ancestor of modern Uzbek and Uyghur. It is written right to left in the Perso-Arabic script. The Latin shown follows the usual Turkological spelling. No one speaks it today, so the sounds are reconstructed.","rows":[["a / ä","back \"a\" (ah) and front \"ä\" (like \"a\" in \"cat\")","at, käl"],["ı / i","ı is a short, flat \"uh\" in the back of the mouth, i is \"ee\"","qız, kiši"],["o / u","back rounded vowels","oq, uč"],["ö / ü","front rounded vowels, as in German \"ö\" and \"ü\"","köz, kün"],["q","a \"k\" made far back in the throat, in back-vowel words","qız"],["k","a plain \"k\", in front-vowel words","kün"],["ğ / g","a soft, throaty \"gh\" and a hard \"g\"","tağ, gül"],["ng","like \"ng\" in \"sing\" (it has its own letter)","täng"],["č / š","\"ch\" and \"sh\"","čay, kiši"]],"notes":["Vowel harmony: back vowels (a, ı, o, u) go with q and ğ. Front vowels (ä, i, ö, ü) go with k and g. Suffixes follow the same rule.","The script writes only some vowels, so the same letters can be read several ways. The script shown is a guide to the spelling, not a standard.","This pool is much smaller than the Spanish one on purpose. Only well-attested forms are included, and they have not been checked by a native reader. Treat it as a first guide."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["one","bir","","بیر"],["two","ikki","","ایکی"],["three","üč","","اوچ"],["four","tört","","تورت"],["five","bäš","","بش"],["six","altı","","آلتی"],["seven","yeti","","یتی"],["eight","säkiz","","سکز"],["nine","toquz","","توقوز"],["ten","on","","اون"]]},{"id":"tens-and-big-numbers","title":"Tens and big numbers","items":[["twenty","yigirmä","","یگرمه"],["thirty","ottuz","","اوتوز"],["forty","qırq","","قیرق"],["fifty","älig","","الیگ"],["one hundred","yüz","","یوز"],["one thousand","ming","","مینگ"]]},{"id":"order-and-amounts","title":"Order and amounts","items":[[